<article>

<h1>How to do batch image processing on the Linux Destop with XnConvert</h1=
>

<div id=3D"ezoic-pub-ad-placeholder-106"></div>



<div class=3D"contributeEdit" style=3D"float:right; width:450px; max-width:=
100%;">
		<div id=3D"tocContainer">
		<h3>On this page</h3>
	<ol class=3D"toc">
<li><a href=3D"https://www.howtoforge.com/tutorial/how-to-do-batch-image-pr=
ocessing-on-linux-with-xnconvert/#-add-your-image-files">1.) Add your image=
 files</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://www.howtoforge.com/tutorial/how-to-do-batch-image-pr=
ocessing-on-linux-with-xnconvert/#-set-actions">2.) Set actions</a></li>
<li><a href=3D"https://www.howtoforge.com/tutorial/how-to-do-batch-image-pr=
ocessing-on-linux-with-xnconvert/#-define-the-output">3.) Define the output=
</a></li>
</ol>
	</div>
	</div>



<p>GIMP is great and I use it all the time, but when it comes to batch imag=
e processing on Linux, nothing is more handy and simple to use than XnConve=
rt. Although not an open source software, this batch raster graphics editor=
 comes for free without any limitations for private use, and works in all p=
latforms and architectures. You can get it from the official website as a c=
omplementary part of XnView (it's standalone). Here comes a tutorial on how=
 to use this simple yet powerful tool on Linux.</p><div style=3D"width:336p=
x;float:left; margin: 10px 15px 10px 0px;background-color:white;"><!-- begi=
n ad tag Rectangle A -->
<!-- /1030080/howtoforge_com_article_rectangle_a_300x250 -->
<div id=3D"div-gpt-ad-1527185271042-6" style=3D"height:250px; width:300px;"=
>

</div>
<!-- End ad tag Rectangle A --></div>
<p></p>
<h2 id=3D"-add-your-image-files">1.) Add your image files</h2>
<p>First you need to add the set of your image files. You may either add fi=
les individually, or choose a folder that contains the images you want to e=
dit. XnConvert boasts a step-by-step GUI approach, so you just have to clic=
k on the first tab named =E2=80=9CInput=E2=80=9D and start adding the files=
 by pressing the corresponding button located on the bottom left. Then you =
may simply navigate your folders and choose the files you want to insert in=
to the software.</p>
<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><a class=3D"fancybox" id=3D"img-pic_1" hre=
f=3D"https://www.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-=
linux-with-xnconvert/big/pic_1.jpg"><img loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"https://ww=
w.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-linux-with-xnco=
nvert/pic_1.jpg" alt=3D"Add image files in XnConvert." width=3D"550" height=
=3D"309"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"-set-actions">2.) Set actions</h2>
<p>The next step is to define what (editing) actions will be performed to e=
ach of the images you have added. Press the =E2=80=9CAdd action=E2=80=9D bu=
tton and choose one action from the list.</p>
<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><a class=3D"fancybox" id=3D"img-pic_2" hre=
f=3D"https://www.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-=
linux-with-xnconvert/big/pic_2.jpg"><img loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"https://ww=
w.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-linux-with-xnco=
nvert/pic_2.jpg" alt=3D"Set Action part 1 in XnConvert." width=3D"550" heig=
ht=3D"309"></a></p>
<p>Once you do that, a new set of options in regards to the added action wi=
ll be given on the left side, while on the right you can see a preview of a=
ny of the added images for your convenience. Here, I use a set of screensho=
ts taken on Virtual Machine, so I first need to crop them.</p>
<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><a class=3D"fancybox" id=3D"img-pic_3" hre=
f=3D"https://www.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-=
linux-with-xnconvert/big/pic_3.jpg"><img loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"https://ww=
w.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-linux-with-xnco=
nvert/pic_3.jpg" alt=3D"Set Actions step 2 in XnConvert." width=3D"550" hei=
ght=3D"309"></a></p>
<p>Among the many actions available in XnConvert, I have found and added a =
watermark. I can change its position, opacity, size etc. All actions in XnC=
onvert feature a wide adjustment range of options. You may add up to 80 act=
ions on this step, but note that batch processing may take quite a while if=
 you act overwhelmingly.</p>
<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><a class=3D"fancybox" id=3D"img-pic_4" hre=
f=3D"https://www.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-=
linux-with-xnconvert/big/pic_4.jpg"><img loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"https://ww=
w.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-linux-with-xnco=
nvert/pic_4.jpg" alt=3D"Set actions step 3 in XnConvert." width=3D"550" hei=
ght=3D"309"></a></p>
<h2 id=3D"-define-the-output">3.) Define the output</h2>
<p>Proceeding to the =E2=80=9COutput=E2=80=9D tab and the final stage of th=
e procedure. Here we can set the output destination, set file naming option=
s, and choose the desired output format. The output options include both lo=
cal options such as =E2=80=9Csave to source folder=E2=80=9D, or directly cr=
eate a zip onto a set disk location, and online service options like upload=
ing to Google's Picasa, or on your Flickr account.</p>
<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><a class=3D"fancybox" id=3D"img-pic_5" hre=
f=3D"https://www.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-=
linux-with-xnconvert/big/pic_5.jpg"><img loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"https://ww=
w.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-linux-with-xnco=
nvert/pic_5.jpg" alt=3D"Define output in XnConvert." width=3D"550" height=
=3D"309"></a></p>
<p>The choice format is a sector where XnConvert naturally shines. You may =
choose among a list of more than five hundred different image formats for t=
he resulting files. All proprietary software formats from Adobe, Autodesk, =
Corel, Canon, Nokia, Kodak, Macintosh, Microsoft, Olympus, Sony, and many m=
ore are supported, so this tool can be a lifesaver as a format converter an=
yway.</p>
<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><a class=3D"fancybox" id=3D"img-pic_6" hre=
f=3D"https://www.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-=
linux-with-xnconvert/big/pic_6.jpg"><img loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"https://ww=
w.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-linux-with-xnco=
nvert/pic_6.jpg" alt=3D"Select Output Format in XnConvert." width=3D"550" h=
eight=3D"309"></a></p>
<p>Last but not least, you may hop on the =E2=80=9CSettings=E2=80=9D tab an=
d change the GUI language. You will be happy to see that there is support f=
or over twenty languages.</p>
<p style=3D"text-align: center;"><a class=3D"fancybox" id=3D"img-pic_7" hre=
f=3D"https://www.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-=
linux-with-xnconvert/big/pic_7.jpg"><img loading=3D"lazy" src=3D"https://ww=
w.howtoforge.com/images/how-to-do-batch-image-processing-on-linux-with-xnco=
nvert/pic_7.jpg" alt=3D"XnConvert settings tab." width=3D"550" height=3D"30=
9"></a></p>
</article>


